      "problem": "Consider a $4\\times 3$ matrix $A.$ If $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A))=1,$ then $\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}({A}^{T}))=$",
      "worked_solution": "The four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14247906/assets/889b83fadd26b377c4dd34143c9b16541630c04e90de3d9b005e7147ead75ad5.png)\n\nIn our case, we are given that $m=4,$ $n=3,$ and $n-r=1.$ \n\nSubstituting $n=3$ into $n-r=1,$ we can find the rank of $A:$\n\n$$\n(3)-r=1\\,⟹\\,r=2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}({A}^{T})) & =r=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
